 is **successful in**.","Choose **in**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"“successful at” does not form the required combination for this action complement.","B":"“successful by” does not form the required combination for this action complement.","C":"“successful from” does not form the required combination for this action complement.","D":"In this sense, the combination is **successful in**."}}},{"id":"6463d8b2-074d-5ee3-a37b-d225f1eaba25","topicSlug":"dependent-prepositions","level":"B1","prompt":"“The team is successful in ____ the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"preparing"},{"id":"B","text":"prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"D","text":"prepared"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Here **in** is a preposition, so the verb after it takes **-ing**.","explanation":"After preposition **in**, use **preparing** to name the action.","keywords":["preposition-gerund"],"skill":"preposition-gerund","contextKey":"dependent-prepositions-49","fingerprint":"d7f0e2b659b9c1e462e5db88a70ecf90f92abc08db23c5c5445efc27ae6fae99","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-dependent-prepositions"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Here **in** is a preposition, so the verb after it takes **-ing**.","After preposition **in**, use **preparing** to name the action.","Choose **preparing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"After preposition **in**, use **preparing** to name the action.","B":"The base verb cannot serve as the action complement after this preposition.","C":"A to-infinitive does not fit after this dependent preposition.","D":"A past form does not name the action after the preposition."}}},{"id":"0cd0473b-caff-5ea5-a347-e28f95b9d98b","topicSlug":"dependent-prepositions","level":"B1","prompt":"Ask about the activity of preparing the evening meal: “What is the team successful ____?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"at"},{"id":"B","text":"by"},{"id":"C","text":"from"},{"id":"D","text":"in"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Question order does not remove the preposition selected by the adjective.","explanation":"The question retains **successful in**, leaving the preposition at the end.","keywords":["question-preposition"],"skill":"question-preposition","contextKey":"dependent-prepositions-49","fingerprint":"5a9387f10eb26ed82ae774d5e5c5fb8268757937247983a09c8c47248e54c4b2","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-dependent-prepositions"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Question order does not remove the preposition selected by the adjective.","The question retains **successful in**, leaving the preposition at the end.","Choose **in**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The question still needs the preposition in “successful in”; changing it to at breaks that combination.","B":"The question still needs the preposition in “successful in”; changing it to by breaks that combination.","C":"The question still needs the preposition in “successful in”; changing it to from breaks that combination.","D":"The question retains **successful in**, leaving the preposition at the end."}}},{"id":"66788f7d-eddc-55d1-aa67-4908d7c8143e","topicSlug":"dependent-prepositions","level":"B1","prompt":"Complete the relative clause about preparing the evening meal: “This is the activity that the team is successful ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"at"},{"id":"B","text":"by"},{"id":"C","text":"in"},{"id":"D","text":"—"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The relative clause must preserve the **whole adjective + preposition** pattern.","explanation":"The relative clause still needs **successful in**; that represents the activity governed by the preposition.","keywords":["relative-preposition"],"skill":"relative-preposition","contextKey":"dependent-prepositions-49","fingerprint":"7e7658ae4b30d0696304939a518df9a1846a55001da6576f82318cc7714eba8d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-dependent-prepositions"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The relative clause must preserve the **whole adjective + preposition** pattern.","The relative clause still needs **successful in**; that represents the activity governed by the preposition.","Choose **in**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The adjective takes in in this sense, not at.","B":"The adjective takes in in this sense, not by.","C":"The relative clause still needs **successful in**; that represents the activity governed by the preposition.","D":"Omitting the relative object does not license omitting its required preposition."}}},{"id":"54c4bb79-c878-52ac-af7d-1dcc1405afca","topicSlug":"dependent-prepositions","level":"B1","prompt":"“The team is tired ____ repairing the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"by"},{"id":"B","text":"from"},{"id":"C","text":"of"},{"id":"D","text":"at"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Recall the complete expression with **tired**, rather than a spatial rule.","explanation":"In this sense, the combination is **tired of**.","keywords":["dependent-combination"],"skill":"dependent-combination","contextKey":"dependent-prepositions-50","fingerprint":"6b0aa68b0d22ead15989f90e2f30dcd955b9356ae0af136d3c37e3b16763f3d1","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-dependent-prepositions"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Recall the complete expression with **tired**, rather than a spatial rule.","In this sense, the combination is **tired of**.","Choose **of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"“tired by” does not form the required combination for this action complement.","B":"“tired from” does not form the required combination for this action complement.","C":"In this sense, the combination is **tired of**.","D":"“tired at” does not form the required combination for this action complement."}}},{"id":"82d42afd-271a-5940-ac92-5f23647330d6","topicSlug":"dependent-prepositions","level":"B1","prompt":"“The team is tired of ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"repair"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Here **of** is a preposition, so the verb after it takes **-ing**.","explanation":"After preposition **of**, use **repairing** to name the action.","keywords":["preposition-gerund"],"skill":"preposition-gerund","contextKey":"dependent-prepositions-50","fingerprint":"bb18dd5abacb5c9fb71aa55ea2e9362c79ea3e241e85fece131f5c7ac13bbdc7","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-dependent-prepositions"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Here **of** is a preposition, so the verb after it takes **-ing**.","After preposition **of**, use **repairing** to name the action.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A to-infinitive does not fit after this dependent preposition.","B":"A past form does not name the action after the preposition.","C":"After preposition **of**, use **repairing** to name the action.","D":"The base verb cannot serve as the action complement after this preposition."}}},{"id":"0fd54f6e-798c-5042-a6c2-60daba620a3e","topicSlug":"dependent-prepositions","level":"B1","prompt":"Ask about the activity of repairing the rowing boat: “What is the team tired ____?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"at"},{"id":"B","text":"by"},{"id":"C","text":"from"},{"id":"D","text":"of"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Question order does not remove the preposition selected by the adjective.","explanation":"The question retains **tired of**, leaving the preposition at the end.","keywords":["question-preposition"],"skill":"question-preposition","contextKey":"dependent-prepositions-50","fingerprint":"20121a430cc0998d44c373913900873ce262a5e3a8b52f13b4f586613ab06095","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-dependent-prepositions"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Question order does not remove the preposition selected by the adjective.","The question retains **tired of**, leaving the preposition at the end.","Choose **of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The question still needs the preposition in “tired of”; changing it to at breaks that combination.","B":"The question still needs the preposition in “tired of”; changing it to by breaks that combination.","C":"The question still needs the preposition in “tired of”; changing it to from breaks that combination.","D":"The question retains **tired of**, leaving the preposition at the end."}}},{"id":"69130195-f88f-540a-a646-ac0f9cf9bbd0","topicSlug":"dependent-prepositions","level":"B1","prompt":"Complete the relative clause about repairing the rowing boat: “This is the activity that the team is tired ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"of"},{"id":"B","text":"—"},{"id":"C","text":"at"},{"id":"D","text":"by"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The relative clause must preserve the **whole adjective + preposition** pattern.","explanation":"The relative clause still needs **tired of**; that represents the activity governed by the preposition.","keywords":["relative-preposition"],"skill":"relative-preposition","contextKey":"dependent-prepositions-50","fingerprint":"b0c797d3136d343405a603c0922f10837745edabcd5f3be9b7d48073c63413d9","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-dependent-prepositions"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The relative clause must preserve the **whole adjective + preposition** pattern.","The relative clause still needs **tired of**; that represents the activity governed by the preposition.","Choose **of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The relative clause still needs **tired of**; that represents the activity governed by the preposition.","B":"Omitting the relative object does not license omitting its required preposition.","C":"The adjective takes of in this sense, not at.","D":"The adjective takes of in this sense, not by."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Learn prepositions as part of a phrase, and use the right complement after them.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Learn complete combinations","explanation":"An adjective or verb often selects a particular preposition: interested in, responsible for, depend on. These are lexical patterns rather than meanings predictable from spatial in/on/at alone. Record the phrase with a short example and its intended sense.","pattern":"**interested in** music / **depend on** support","commonMistakes":["Some words allow different prepositions with different meanings; angry with a person and angry about an event are both possible."],"examples":["“The team is interested **in** preparing the visitor guide.”","“The team is responsible **for** restoring the old portrait.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Prepositions take noun-like complements","explanation":"After a preposition, use a noun, object pronoun or -ing clause when naming an action. To can itself be a preposition in expressions such as look forward to, where it is followed by -ing rather than a base verb.","pattern":"look forward to **meeting** you","commonMistakes":["Do not treat every to as an infinitive marker. Its job depends on the preceding expression."],"examples":["“The team is interested in **preparing** the visitor guide.”","“The team is responsible for **restoring** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Questions can leave the preposition at the end","explanation":"A neutral direct question commonly leaves a dependent preposition near the end of its clause. Formal English can place it before whom or which, but this changes register and is not always natural with every multi-word expression.","pattern":"What are you **interested in**?","commonMistakes":["Ending an English sentence with a preposition is often entirely grammatical."],"examples":["Ask about the activity of preparing the visitor guide: “What is the team interested **in**?”","Ask about the activity of restoring the old portrait: “What is the team responsible **for**?”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Keep the preposition in relative clauses","explanation":"When a relative clause contains a dependent combination, preserve the preposition even if an object relative pronoun is omitted. A formal fronted preposition requires whom or which, not that.","pattern":"the subject **(that) she is interested in**","commonMistakes":["The subject which she is interested omits the preposition required by interested in."],"examples":["Complete the relative clause about preparing the visitor guide: “This is the activity that the team is interested **in**.”","Complete the relative clause about restoring the old portrait: “This is the activity that the team is responsible **for**.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**interested in** music / **depend on** support","look forward to **meeting** you","What are you **interested in**?","the subject **(that) she is interested in**"]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["verb-patterns"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
